<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Allowlist. Overrides the permissive include-everything default in the shared :library
  module. Both sections use <include>, so anything not named here is excluded.

  ALLOWED: shared_prefs/email_settings.xml
      Holds one key, "load_remote_images" (EmailSettings.kt). It is an app-wide privacy
      preference with no account scope, no PII and no crypto dependency, and it is the one
      setting a user would notice silently reverting after a restore. The cloud-backup
      section sets disableIfNoEncryptionCapabilities="true" so even this only leaves the
      device under Google's client-side end-to-end encryption.

  EXCLUDED (by omission from the allowlist):

    databases/email-db (+ -wal/-shm/-journal), databases/email-db_temp
        The EmailAccount table holds accessToken and refreshToken as PLAINTEXT columns
        (EmailModels.kt:88-89) alongside passwordEncrypted/passwordIv wrapped by the
        AndroidKeyStore alias "email_account_pw". The same table holds every account's
        server config, and the EmailMessage table holds all cached message bodies. Account
        config cannot be separated from the tokens: they are columns on the same row, in
        the same file. The file is SQLCipher-encrypted under a passphrase wrapped by
        "db_no_auth_key", and neither Keystore alias survives cloud backup or D2D, so a
        restored database is unreadable as well as unsafe. email-db_temp is the plaintext
        staging file from the plaintext->SQLCipher migration.
    shared_prefs/secure_prefs.xml
        Wrapped SQLCipher passphrase + IV. Restoring it without its Keystore key leaves
        DatabaseHelper.isKeyGenerated() reporting a key that no longer exists.
    shared_prefs/outlook_oauth.xml
        In-flight PKCE "verifier" and "state" plus "emailHint". A restored verifier/state
        pair is a stale CSRF token for an authorization request that no longer exists, and
        emailHint is an account address. No value, real risk.
    shared_prefs/email_notif_last_seen.xml
        Keys are literally "<accountEmail>::<folderName>", so the file enumerates the
        user's email addresses. The only benefit is suppressing one burst of duplicate
        notifications, and since the accounts database is not restored there is nothing
        for those UIDs to line up against.
    shared_prefs/email_idle_state.xml
        "idle_restart_pending" is transient runtime state. Restoring it true would enqueue
        an IMAP IDLE restart on a device with no configured accounts.
    files/outbox/<entryId>/ and files/outbox/<entryId>/inline/
        Attachment and inline-image payloads for queued outgoing mail, stored as plaintext
        files. Unsent user mail content; must not be uploaded.
    files/ Glance widget state
        androidx.glance.appwidget writes per-widget-id state files under files/. Widget ids
        are not stable across installs, so the state is meaningless after a restore, and
        the widget caches message subjects and senders. Regenerable.
    databases/androidx.work.workdb (+ sidecars)
        WorkManager scheduling state, including serialized worker input data. Regenerable.
    cache/cid/, cache/eml_cid/, cache/inline/, cache/eml_attachments/
        Decoded inline images and attachments from received mail. The framework never
        backs up cacheDir, but they are named here so the audit is complete.

  Device transfer carries the same single preference. It is not widened relative to cloud
  backup, because D2D does not migrate AndroidKeyStore material and the accounts database
  would arrive unopenable.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup disableIfNoEncryptionCapabilities="true">
        <include domain="sharedpref" path="email_settings.xml" />
    </cloud-backup>
    <device-transfer>
        <include domain="sharedpref" path="email_settings.xml" />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
